00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.149 align:start position:0%
 
we try to look at every aspect of our  life that's changed because of the  pandemic and one of the biggest is how  we work those of us lucky enough to  still have a job have had to adapt we're  getting the work done from our kitchens  or our couches pajamas are the new  business suit what's that done though to  the millions of square meters of office  space around the world much of it is  empty now and it could stay that way for  a long time  Jackson Prosecco explores the future of  offices in a building in Falls Church  Virginia  we found what may be the workplace of  the future an office retrofitted and  ready for the era of kovat 19 at least  that's the hope people have seen some of  the things that we're doing and they  want to incorporate it into their  offices as well christian Pinkston has  been working to get his company prepared  for it's uncertain next steps figuring  out what it will take to welcome staff  back into the office the concept of just  a deeper cleaning once or twice a day  isn't going to be enough and because  once you bring people back into the  space it's it's instantly contaminated  again in the US and Canada there are few  guidelines for how offices should  operate in a pandemic  so Kingston developed his own playbook  taking a gamble on technology starting  with this ultraviolet cleanse portal the  new front door they'll go through this  portal kind of slowly they'll pause  they'll do a slow turn they're making  sure that all that you know the surfaces  of their hands and in clothes are  exposed to the UV lighting and so it's  it's cleansing them of any microbes  viruses bacteria they might be carrying  with them and anything they're carrying  as well so if I step into this I mean  I'm not seeing anything there's a little  bit of like a faint a faint light and a  faint sound but that's it that purple  glow is UVC light known for its germ  zapping abilities Pinkston has also  installed UVC fixtures in high-traffic  areas like the kitchen along with  specialized air filters we know that  Karuna virus will spread through  the air droplets and so that's a major  risk factor and so we've installed an  air sanitizing troffer in this room  that's constantly recirculating the air  and in with a very powerful UVC light  inside of it then there are the  invisible changes special sensors detect  if there's virus in the air every  surface is now covered in an  antimicrobial coating of titanium  dioxide we wanted to do something that  was realistic financially but we wanted  to do as much as we could within that  parameter to provide the safest possible  environment the reality is that offices  are the kind of places where kovat 19  can thrive full of shared surfaces close  quarters and long days of uncontrolled  contact researchers in South Korea  discovered how easily the virus can  spread in that environment  after a cluster of 97 employees at a  call center in Seoul tested positive for  kovat 19 when health officials looked  closer they found 94 of them work  together on the same floor and 79 of  them sat in the same section of the  office nearly half of the workers on  that one floor ended up getting sick to  make sure that doesn't happen again  offices and office buildings will have  to take drastic steps from top to bottom  professor Joseph Allen is an expert in  healthy buildings we have to employ some  of these healthy building strategies  like bringing more fresh outdoor air to  dilute the airborne contaminants and  filter the recirculated air using  high-efficiency filters that's just one  small part of a multi-layered defense  desks will have to be spaced at least  two metres apart high-walled cubicles  may have to make a comeback employees  and visitors will be subjected to  temperature checks don't be surprised if  everyone has to wear a mask kitchens and  common areas will likely be off-limits  even the little things like pictures and  trinkets on desks will likely disappear  to make way for daily deep cleaning if  you layer enough defenses on top of each  other you make it really difficult in  this case of security  for a bad actor to penetrate your system  right same thing with this virus if you  layer enough of these defenses on top of  each other you don't eliminate risk  there's no such thing as zero risk you  get it to a point where it can become  manageable and to a level where people  can feel comfortable coming back into  your building the elevator may be the  biggest hurdle there's just no way  around it expect a limit on how many  people can ride at a time and then  imagine what that means in a building  like New York's One World Trade Center  where 8,000 workers are spread across 90  floors and so the easiest solution may  just be for companies to keep some  people working from home staggering  shifts or alternating days to have fewer  people in the office at any given time I  think one thing that it has taught  people is that you can remote you can  work remotely and you can do it  effectively now the things you can't do  effectively I think that's what we're  gonna lean on the office for Albert day  plays Ola is the director of global  strategy for the design firm unis base  he sees kovat 19 changing the role of  the office forever Canadian tech giant  Shopify has already declared the era of  office centricity is over employees will  stay working from home until 2021 after  that the office will be for specific  uses only not an everyday destination  twitter has gone even further telling  staff they can work from home forever if  they want to now we've done a ton of  surveys with organizations we're getting  a lot of we get we're getting a lot of  feedback and input from CFOs and what  we're seeing is that at least forty to  sixty percent of employees want to work  from home even if there is a vaccine one  two three days a week for all the  precautions he's taken Christian  Pinkston has found there's not actually  much enthusiasm among his staff for  returning to the office and we did an  anonymous survey of our staff a couple  of weeks ago and I was shocked by what I  found  they were not ready to come back and  there was still a lot of uncertainty a  lot of fear there's very much still  avoiding any unnecessary contact with  people that aren't you know family any  plan to get employees back to their  desks will have to include plenty of  reassurance a communication plan to let  people know what's being done to keep  them safe and flexibility to let them  decide when they're ready to come back  once we're opening the office to staff  it'll be on a as you're comfortable  basis will be sending out alerts when  clients or Outsiders will be in the  office we're going to be having staff  monitoring their temperatures and  symptoms and making decisions on a  day-by-day basis as to whether it's safe  for them to come in so lots of  communication and some confusion for  both employers and employees about when  to go back and how to go back who needs  a shake is a labor and employment lawyer  with Levitt LLP and she's in Richmond  Hill Ontario  manisa thanks for joining us returning  to work in a pandemic is new ground for  all of us what obligations broadly do  employers have when it comes to bringing  staff back into the office can they just  say we need you back and expect you to  show up well they can certainly say say  to their employees that they want you  back and we need you back but ultimately  whether or not employees have an  obligation to show up is largely  dependent on how safe the workplace is  now I will tell you that employers have  to follow much of the very same  guidelines that we're watching on TV as  they come down the pipeline through the  provincial government and direction from  the federal government around social  distancing where possible and sometimes  with certain jobs as you know it isn't  possible and providing appropriate  protective gear when social distancing  is not possible so unless employers  really understand that they need to step  up to the challenge and do their best to  keep their employees safe simply telling  them to return to return to work given  this current pandemic is not enough  what about employee rights can people be  forced back to the office if they feel  it's not safe where they're  uncomfortable about  so while your employer has an obligation  to keep you safe and healthy in the  workplace an employer does not have an  obligation to accommodate your stress  worrier angst because let's be honest  given the nature of this pandemic and  given much of what we've come to learn  in the past few weeks and months you  know there is no guarantee that you will  be fully insulated from this virus so as  long as your employer meets that  reasonable threshold in terms of keeping  you safe whether it's through social  distancing or protective gear may be  working from remotely in a staggered way  a lot of companies are doing that I know  our firm is doing that unless you know  as long as they are meeting that  threshold simply telling your employer  that look I understand that you're doing  all of these things but I'm still  worried I still don't want to come into  the workplace that simply is not good  enough from the employee perspective and  you could very well be faced with the  risk of being told by your employer that  because we have done everything that we  understand we need to do your refusal to  come into work at this point would  either be considered to be a resignation  or a job abandonment if that reasonable  threshold isn't reached though if  employees find that hygiene is  insufficient or physical distancing is  lacks are they stuck then or what can  they reasonably do at that point  they absolutely aren't stuck although I  can appreciate from a positional power  perspective that it's really difficult  to walk up to your employer and say hey  you know you're saying you're doing the  right things but I'm actually here and  it seems like you aren't in those  circumstances the employees most  certainly can file a complaint under the  Occupational Health and Safety Act I  know some of the Labor Relations  officers or ministry of labor officers  are responding pretty promptly to these  complaints that can be made anonymously  and they will come and check the  workplace out to see if indeed it's only  lip service or you know has the employer  actually implemented you know the social  distancing the protective gears and in  some cases it might be something as  simple as you know putting up signs and  reminders to employees you know in the  lunchroom perhaps or other areas where  employees tend to congregate  reminding them that social distancing is  to be observed at all times let's talk  about parents working from home who are  juggling work duties and looking after  young kids if they don't have access to  childcare schools not back for example  is that a legitimate reason to say I  can't come back to the office yet  it absolutely is a legitimate reason  under the human rights legislation there  is a protected ground which speaks to an  accommodation as far as it might relate  to family status and so if you as an  employer and many of my clients I've  said this to me has you know your  employees who are parents calling you  and saying look I understand that you're  not offering a remote option but under  no circumstances can I come back into  the workplace because I don't have  childcare in those circumstances what  you could expect is a job protective  leave that would be unpaid so your job  is safe  but the employer has no obligation to  pay you if you are home with your kids  this is as I said new ground for all of  us do you expect that there'll be lots  of test cases of employment law as this  pandemic continues absolutely and what  we have been saying to our clients  whether they are employer clients or  whether they're employee quiet clients  is this is unchartered territory for so  many of us including employment lawyers  so notwithstanding the fact that you  know we are giving certain legal advice  we've seen we've seen just a couple  weeks ago certain legislative changes  and I think some of these test cases  from an employment law perspective in  the coming months are really going to  map out for us what an accommodation  looks like what the employers  obligations are during a pandemic and  most importantly what is that reasonable  test in terms of keeping employees safe  in the workplace and those are specific  areas where I expect there to be a  significant amount of legal narrative  which could very well change the advice  some of the advice that we give to our  clients  [Music]

